inkled throughout that  I thought I wonder if that happened to  her in real life like if somebody says  they're drinking Linda what does that  really mean well many people when  they've been the victim of a crime  want to minimize they want you to think  better of them in law enforcement so  they'll often say I had a drink I had a  drink and a half and you talked to the  bartender says she knocked down four  drinks or three eat the question after  you asked oh if I repeat the question if  I repeat the question after I'm trying  to think of an answer don't be the best  answer so I turned those again around on  her how exchanged so nine put nineteen  books later I'm actually 24 year old 24  years older than when I first created  her she's aged 3 years which is pretty  nice  so physically it's been a 35 she's only  38 but she's learned from the  experiences it's not easy to work with  victims of violent violent crime and  especially in sexual assault domestic  violence areas so you see that change in  her throughout several books ago she was  kidnapped obviously got out of it fine  but she's had some PTSD that she's dealt  with and this event sort of brings her  in Deadpool back to me it's not easy to  to war to work with it it also can't be  easy to write about it a lot of times I  think that's a good question definite I  didn't view it originally as being  cathartic but it is and it was such a  dark world when Charlie first talked to  me you know people really thought what  do you do why do you do this work it  can't be rewarding it's just dark and  for me it was richly rewarding because  we were getting for the first time  justice in court for victims of this  kind of intimate violence so to write  about it I mean I think helped me work  through a lot of the issues and then  give it to the reader how do you get  better at what you do do you read other  very good crime writers and if so who  are they yes I read em all the time  Harlan Coben is a good friend of the  show and Lee Child and Mary Higgins  Clark and Lisa scottoline II David  Baldacci probably changed my writing  style without knowing it how did he do  that I'd written three books my late  husband and I had lunch with him and in  prep for the lunch my husband raced  through a bunch of his books and he said  David you and every chapter with the  tea's you make me want a page turn at  the end of every chapter I can't go to  sleep that kind of  writers dream I said maybe maybe it's  something I ought to do so I credit  David Baldacci style for getting me to  to not end with a funny word exactly  what you did in this book I know the  question yeah she ends with something  that you want to know what's gonna  happen so even if you want to stop you  go well now I got to figure now I got to  keep going cuz I want to know what's  gonna happen they called you hell on  heels why well that was a I think a  compliment at the time I didn't much  think so but the Daily News did it was  not what my colleagues did but once that  headline ran stuff there was a lot of  teasing and that was fun but there's a  30-year career long career so why does  that mean because you're tough because  what tough was important to be I didn't  go there as a kid at a law school  thinking I would ever be tough thinking  this would be a prosecutorial career  fair was the most important thing so  that combination the heels were good at  the time as you know I love the Sunday  morning peace on you where you said it  dreamed to have a career I wanted all my  life because writing who would think  that this would turn out so that's what  I wanted my dad used to say get a grip  you know you need a career get a job you  can't sit in a garret writing books all  day and this great career in the law  gave me deadfall gave me books thank you  to you all thank you good Thank You  Linda fair Stein

